          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What TSH range does guidance describe as the treatment threshold in subclinical hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "Guidance indicates that treatment for subclinical hypothyroidism is typically considered when the thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) level is persistently above about 10 mIU/L. For TSH levels between the upper reference limit and 10 mIU/L, guidance varies. Some recommend a trial of treatment if symptoms, positive antibodies, or cardiovascular risk are present, while others suggest monitoring instead. This information comes from the \"Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance (educational summary).\"\n\nI could not find specific ranges for TSH levels under 10 mIU/L or detailed treatment protocols beyond this threshold.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
